  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the architecture and operating principle of a tandem perovskite solar cell. Two light-absorbing layers are stacked: a wide-bandgap perovskite on top and a narrow-bandgap perovskite below, with charge-selective transport layers, electrodes, and an internal recombination junction between them. Sunlight enters from the top, and the upper absorber takes higher-energy light while longer-wavelength light passes deeper to the lower absorber. In each perovskite, absorbed light creates electron–hole pairs, which are then separated and guided by electron- and hole-extraction layers toward the appropriate interfaces.\n\nA dipolar surfactant is shown near the middle of the stack, suggesting an interfacial modification that helps charge separation or alignment at the junction between the two subcells. The diagram emphasizes that charges generated in the two absorbers are collected and combined to produce current at the external electrodes.\n\nThe inset explains the electronic idea behind this device performance: useful operation depends on exciting electrons across the bandgap to leave holes behind, whereas defect states within the bandgap can enable non-radiative recombination, causing electrons and holes to recombine without producing useful electrical output. Overall, the figure links multilayer device design with reduced recombination and improved current generation.",
